.TH CRYPTOSEC 1 "2026-09-22" "cryptosec 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cryptosec \- encrypt and decrypt files or directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cryptosec
.B \-e
.I PATH
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cryptosec
.B \-d
.I PATH
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B cryptosec
.BR keygen | keys | open
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cryptosec
turns a file or a whole directory into a single encrypted container with the
extension
.IR .csec .
A directory is packed into a tar archive first, so the tree, its permissions
and its symlinks survive the round trip.
.PP
Encrypting replaces the input and decrypting replaces the container, so a
plaintext copy is not left next to its encrypted version. The source is only
removed after the finished container has been read back and compared against
it, byte for byte for a file and entry by entry for a directory. Use
.B \-k
to keep the input instead.
.PP
Every container starts with three readable lines that are identical in every
container: the words
.BR "Encrypted by CryptoSec" ,
the command needed to unpack it, and the project URL. Nothing in the readable
part describes the individual file.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-algo
the scheme is picked from a menu. The choices are AES\-256\-GCM and
ChaCha20\-Poly1305 with a password, and RSA\-4096, X25519 or ML\-KEM\-768 with a
key pair. The key pair schemes never encrypt the payload directly; they wrap a
random 256 bit data key that AES\-256\-GCM then uses for the payload.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt " " \fIPATH\fR
Encrypt a file or directory.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decrypt " " \fIPATH\fR
Decrypt a container.
.TP
.BI \-\-info " PATH"
Print what a container holds without decrypting it.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIPATH\fR
Write the result here instead of next to the input.
.TP
.BI \-\-algo " SCHEME"
One of
.BR aes ", " chacha ", " rsa ", " ecc ", " mlkem .
Skips the menu.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " NAME"
Key pair from the key store, or a path to a key file.
.TP
.B \-\-password\-stdin
Read the password from standard input instead of prompting. Intended for
scripts; the password may end up in your shell history.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing output path.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep
Leave the input in place instead of replacing it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the read\-back check that normally runs after encrypting. The input is
then kept, because nothing was verified.
.TP
.B \-\-shred
Overwrite the source with random bytes before removing it. Best effort only;
see
.BR "SECURITY NOTES" .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print errors only.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B keygen [NAME] [\-t TYPE]
Create a key pair.
.I TYPE
is
.BR rsa ", " ecc " or " mlkem .
The private key is protected with a passphrase unless
.B \-\-no\-passphrase
is given.
.TP
.B keys
List the key pairs in the key store.
.TP
.B open PATH
Show a container and offer to decrypt it. This is what the desktop file
handler calls when a
.I .csec
file is opened from a file manager.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cryptosec/keys
Key store.
.I NAME.pub
holds the public key,
.I NAME.key
the private one with mode 0600. Override the location with
.BR CRYPTOSEC_HOME .
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Passwords are stretched with Argon2id (64 MiB, 3 passes, 4 lanes). The payload
is encrypted in 1 MiB chunks, each with its own nonce, and the final chunk is
marked, so truncating a container is detected instead of silently accepted.
.PP
Not hidden by the format: the size of the payload, the scheme in use and, for
key pair containers, the fingerprint of the recipient. No creation time is
stored. The original name is kept inside the encrypted part.
.PP
The salt, the nonces and the wrapped data key sit in the header in the clear,
as they must. A salt is not a secret; it exists to defeat precomputed tables
and to keep two containers sharing a password from being attacked together.
Anything cryptosec can read without a key an attacker can read as well, so
none of it is relied on for secrecy.
.PP
Removing a source unlinks it and leaves the old blocks on the disk until they
are reused.
.B \-\-shred
overwrites the contents once first. On SSDs, copy\-on\-write filesystems such
as Btrfs or ZFS, and anything with snapshots, older copies of the data can
still exist afterwards.
.PP
Losing a private key or forgetting a password means losing the data. There is
no recovery path and none can be added.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error, including a failed authentication check.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cryptosec \-e notes.txt
cryptosec \-e ~/documents
cryptosec \-d notes.txt.csec
cryptosec \-k \-e notes.txt
cryptosec keygen work \-t ecc
cryptosec \-\-algo ecc \-\-key work \-e report.pdf
.fi
